{
  "dom_id": "step-q341907",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Given that $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)))+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$",
